[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: A system is a named set of source directories; membership is computed from a node's path and a node may be in more than one

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-SYSTEM-IS-A-SOURCE-SET
TITLE: A system is a named set of source directories; membership is computed from a node's path and a node may be in more than one
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: A body of nodes must be a system that no set of source directories can name -- membership assigned by hand, or one file holding two systems.
STATEMENT: >>>
The canon is read as SYSTEMS a person can switch on and off, and a system is a
named set of SOURCE DIRECTORIES -- the part of the repository a body of nodes
documents -- never a set of node types.

A system is a row of [LINK: NAR-CANON-SYSTEMS], a durable table in the spec:
its name, the source prefixes it holds, whether it may be switched off, and
what it is for. A node belongs to a system when its file's path begins with
one of that system's prefixes. Membership is computed from the path alone and
is a SET, not a partition: a prefix that contains another puts a node in both.

A node shows when any system holding it is on. An edge shows when both its
ends show. A relation ROLE gates nothing, because a role says what kind of
claim an edge makes and not which part of the repository it belongs to.

A system whose switch reads `always` cannot be turned off. The shared model in
`docs/spec/` is cited by every other system, so hiding it would empty the
screens that present it rather than narrow them.

Switching changes what is shown and never what was rendered: the payload is
the same file whatever the switches say.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
[LINK: DEC-SYSTEM-IS-A-TYPE-SET] read the operator's ask for switchable systems
as a partition of node TYPES and shipped switches named canon, whiteboard and
work. Three things were wrong with it, and only the first is a matter of taste.

It answered the wrong question. The operator's systems are the parts of the
repository being documented -- the canon's tooling, the view that renders it,
beads next, and the rest of the repo after that -- so the axis is the subject,
not the grammar. A type switch cuts ACROSS every one of them: with whiteboard
off, 10 of the view plan's 59 nodes went and every narrative in every unrelated
part of the repository went with them. No combination of the three switches
isolated one part.

It could not name a part of the repository that has no narrative.
`docs/plans/beads-migration/` is 20 nodes and holds no root, so under a type
axis and a root-per-tab strip it was unreachable however the switches were set.

And it took the word canon for a proper subset of the canon, contradicting
[LINK: DEC-CANON-NAMES-THE-COMMITTED-CONTENTS] one level down: switching canon
off did not switch off the canon, and switching it on did not show it.

Membership by path costs nothing. The export already carries every node's file,
and dir_class was already derived from it for the node index's facets, so this
moves an axis the payload had into the switches instead of computing a new one.
It also makes documenting the next part of the repository one row in a table
rather than a change to a script.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Ruled 2026-08-30 as a first-pass decision under the operator's standing
delegation, on their reading of the render: "my intent was there are different
semantic modules (core - cannon, whiteboard - the renderer, beads for future)
... you could turn various projects on and off and get the aggregate of all
those sdocs populating the contents of each tab." Overrule by superseding.

AUTHORED_BY reads llm because the writer has no flag for the human rungs.

This decides the axis only. Which words label a switch is the table's, and the
tab strip that presents the aggregate is
[LINK: DEC-NARRATIVE-DECLARES-ITS-PLACE] and the view renderer's.
[LINK: DEC-SYSTEM-TAG-ON-EVERY-ELEMENT] asked whether a node could name a
system by hand when its TYPE placed it wrongly; under this decision the
question is whether a node can belong to a system its PATH does not place it
in, which the beads import may still raise.
<<<
